4.3 Three-phase Unbalanced System
Using the same KVL we used before, Equation 4.9, we can set a system of equations for buses \(i\) and \(j\) such that \(i\) is upstream of \(j\):
\[
\begin{bmatrix}
\bar{V}^{a}_{j} \\
\bar{V}^{b}_{j} \\
\bar{V}^{c}_{j}
\end{bmatrix}
=
\begin{bmatrix}
\bar{V}^{a}_{i} \\
\bar{V}^{b}_{i} \\
\bar{V}^{c}_{i}
\end{bmatrix}
-
\begin{bmatrix}
\bar{Z}^{aa}_{ij} & \bar{Z}^{ab}_{ij} & \bar{Z}^{ac}_{ij}\\
\bar{Z}^{ba}_{ij} & \bar{Z}^{bb}_{ij} & \bar{Z}^{bc}_{ij}\\
\bar{Z}^{ca}_{ij} & \bar{Z}^{cb}_{ij} & \bar{Z}^{cc}_{ij}
\end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix}
\bar{I}^{a}_{ij} \\
\bar{I}^{b}_{ij} \\
\bar{I}^{c}_{ij}
\end{bmatrix}
\tag{4.14}\]
Note that the diagonal elements of \(\bar{Z}_{ij}\) are self-impedances, and the off-diagonal elements are mutual impedances. This is a three-phase line model; a line with fewer phases uses the appropriate reduced impedance matrix. Neutral and transformer modeling must be handled consistently in the feeder model.
For any given phase, the voltage equation can be extracted from the above matrix form. For instance:
\[\bar{V}^{a}_{j} = \bar{V}^{a}_{i} - \bar{Z}^{aa}_{ij}\bar{I}^{a}_{ij} - \bar{Z}^{ab}_{ij}\bar{I}^{b}_{ij} - \bar{Z}^{ac}_{ij}\bar{I}^{c}_{ij} \tag{4.15}\]
We can rewrite the current terms in Equation 4.15 using complex power for each phase. At the sending end of line \((i,j)\):
\[\bar{S}^{a}_{ij} = \bar{V}^{a}_{i}(\bar{I}^{a}_{ij})^{*} = P^{a}_{ij}+jQ^{a}_{ij}\] \[\bar{S}^{b}_{ij} = \bar{V}^{b}_{i}(\bar{I}^{b}_{ij})^{*} = P^{b}_{ij}+jQ^{b}_{ij}\] \[\bar{S}^{c}_{ij} = \bar{V}^{c}_{i}(\bar{I}^{c}_{ij})^{*} = P^{c}_{ij}+jQ^{c}_{ij}\]
As a vector format, we can express the three complex power equations as:
\[
\begin{bmatrix}
\bar{S}^{a}_{ij} \\
\bar{S}^{b}_{ij} \\
\bar{S}^{c}_{ij}
\end{bmatrix}
=
\begin{bmatrix}
\bar{V}^{a}_{i}(\bar{I}^{a}_{ij})^{*} \\
\bar{V}^{b}_{i}(\bar{I}^{b}_{ij})^{*} \\
\bar{V}^{c}_{i}(\bar{I}^{c}_{ij})^{*}
\end{bmatrix}
=
\begin{bmatrix}
P^{a}_{ij}+jQ^{a}_{ij} \\
P^{b}_{ij}+jQ^{b}_{ij} \\
P^{c}_{ij}+jQ^{c}_{ij}
\end{bmatrix}
\tag{4.16}\]






NoteNote



Equation 4.16 is a \(3\times1\) vector of per-phase complex powers. It is not equal to a \(3\times3\) matrix. The full branch-power matrix used by Gan and Low, including cross-phase products, is discussed in the background file. Mutual coupling is already present in Equation 4.14 through the off-diagonal impedances.



4.3.1 Derive the current equations
Consider the complex power for phase \(a\) between two buses:
\[\bar{S}_{ij}^{a} = \bar{V}_{i}^{a}(\bar{I}_{ij}^{a})^{*}\]
Take the complex conjugate of both sides:
\[\left(\bar{S}_{ij}^{a}\right)^{*} = (\bar{V}_{i}^{a})^{*}\bar{I}_{ij}^{a}\]
Solving for \(\bar{I}_{ij}^{a}\):
\[\bar{I}_{ij}^{a} = \frac{\left(\bar{S}_{ij}^{a}\right)^{*}}{(\bar{V}_{i}^{a})^{*}}\]
Which can be rewritten as:
\[\bar{I}_{ij}^{a} = \frac{P_{ij}^{a} - jQ_{ij}^{a}}{(\bar{V}_{i}^{a})^{*}} \tag{4.17}\]
Solving for phases \(b\) and \(c\) using the same approach:
\[\bar{I}_{ij}^{b} = \frac{P_{ij}^{b} - jQ_{ij}^{b}}{(\bar{V}_{i}^{b})^{*}} \tag{4.18}\]
\[\bar{I}_{ij}^{c} = \frac{P_{ij}^{c} - jQ_{ij}^{c}}{(\bar{V}_{i}^{c})^{*}} \tag{4.19}\]
Equation 4.17, Equation 4.18, and Equation 4.19 can be used in our KVL equation Equation 4.14, such that:
\[
\begin{bmatrix}
\bar V_j^a \\
\bar V_j^b \\
\bar V_j^c
\end{bmatrix}
=
\begin{bmatrix}
\bar V_i^a \\
\bar V_i^b \\
\bar V_i^c
\end{bmatrix}
-
\bar{Z}_{ij}
\begin{bmatrix}
\dfrac{P_{ij}^{a} - jQ_{ij}^{a}}{(\bar{V}_{i}^{a})^{*}} \\
\dfrac{P_{ij}^{b} - jQ_{ij}^{b}}{(\bar{V}_{i}^{b})^{*}} \\
\dfrac{P_{ij}^{c} - jQ_{ij}^{c}}{(\bar{V}_{i}^{c})^{*}}
\end{bmatrix}
\tag{4.20}\]
Equation 4.20 expresses the receiving-end voltage phasors as a function of the sending-end voltage phasors, branch impedance, and phase real and reactive power flows (Gan and Low 2014):
\[\boxed{\mathbf{\bar V}_{j}=\mathbf f(\mathbf{\bar V}_{i},\mathbf{\bar Z}_{ij},\mathbf P_{ij},\mathbf Q_{ij})}\]






NoteNote



This is an exact branch relationship, not yet an explicit voltage-magnitude sensitivity to inverter reactive power injection. To connect an inverter at bus \(k\) to voltages throughout the feeder, we must also use network current/power balance and the inverter injection convention. Gan and Low’s squared-voltage matrix derivation is in the background file for reference.






Gan, Lingwen, and Steven H Low. 2014. “Convex Relaxations and Linear Approximation for Optimal Power Flow in Multiphase Radial Networks.” 2014 Power Systems Computation Conference, 1–9.
